# 2 shared buffers
("""In PostgreSQL, this memory region is called <strong class="text-white">shared buffers</strong>. Every backend process that executes a query shares access to this pool. When PostgreSQL reads a page from disk, it writes it into shared buffers, and subsequent requests for the same page find it there. The default size for shared buffers is 128 MB, a conservative value that almost every production deployment should raise significantly. A common starting point for a dedicated database server is 25% of total system RAM, though workload characteristics ultimately determine the right value.""",
 """In SQL Server, this memory region is the <strong class="text-white">buffer pool</strong>. Every query that reads a page goes through it: when SQL Server reads a page from disk, it loads it into the buffer pool, and subsequent requests for the same page are served from memory. SQL Server manages this memory dynamically — by default it will take nearly all available RAM — so the critical DBA decision is the """ + code('max server memory') + """ setting: cap it explicitly and leave headroom for the OS, the thread stacks, and anything else on the box. On a dedicated database server, reserving 4-8 GB (or roughly ten percent) for the OS and giving the rest to SQL Server is the standard starting point."""),

# 3 LRU both engines
("""Both engines use variations of the <strong class="text-white">LRU (Least Recently Used)</strong> algorithm to decide which pages to evict when the buffer pool fills up. PostgreSQL uses a clock-sweep algorithm, a practical approximation of LRU that avoids the overhead of maintaining a strict ordered list. SQL Server uses a more sophisticated version that also considers page reference counts and system memory pressure signals. In both systems, hot pages — those accessed frequently — tend to stay in memory, while cold pages get pushed out as new data enters.""",
 """SQL Server uses an LRU-K style algorithm to decide which pages to evict when the buffer pool fills up — weighing how recently and how frequently each page was touched, plus system memory pressure signals. Hot pages — those accessed frequently — tend to stay in memory, while cold pages get pushed out as new data enters. When Windows signals memory pressure, SQL Server trims the buffer pool cooperatively rather than fighting the OS for pages."""),

