# 11 plan cache both
("""Parsing, analyzing, and planning a query takes CPU time. For a simple lookup query that executes thousands of times per second, re-planning the query on every execution is wasteful. Both PostgreSQL and SQL Server maintain a plan cache — an in-memory store of compiled query plans — that allows previously seen queries to skip the planning phase entirely.""",
 """Parsing, optimizing, and compiling a query takes CPU time. For a simple lookup query that executes thousands of times per second, re-compiling on every execution is wasteful. SQL Server maintains a plan cache — an in-memory store of compiled query plans — that allows previously seen queries to skip the optimization phase entirely, keyed by the query text hash."""),

# 12 PG prepared statements
("""PostgreSQL handles plan caching differently from SQL Server. In PostgreSQL, query plan caching happens primarily through <strong class="text-white">prepared statements</strong>. When a client prepares a statement using %s or a protocol-level prepare (as most connection poolers and ORMs do), PostgreSQL initially uses a generic plan after enough executions convince it that the generic plan is efficient. For the first five executions of a prepared statement, PostgreSQL creates a custom plan for each execution using the actual parameter values. After five executions, it evaluates whether a generic cached plan would be cheaper on average.""" % code('PREPARE'),
 """SQL Server caches plans for ad-hoc batches, %s calls, and stored procedures alike. Ad-hoc SQL benefits from <strong class="text-white">simple parameterization</strong> — the engine automatically parameterizes trivial queries so that %s and %s share one cached plan. For complex queries, parameterization doesn't happen automatically; that's what %s is for. If your workload is dominated by non-parameterized ad-hoc SQL, the plan cache fills with single-use plans — watch the %s counter, and consider %s at the database level before reaching for trace flags.""" % (code('sp_executesql'), code('SELECT * FROM Orders WHERE OrderId = 1'), code('SELECT * FROM Orders WHERE OrderId = 2'), code('sp_executesql'), code('Cache Hit Ratio'), code('forced parameterization'))),

# 13 plan_cache_mode
("""This behavior — called <strong class="text-white">plan stabilization</strong> — exists because the optimal plan for %s might differ from %s depending on data distribution. A generic plan must work reasonably well for all parameter values. The %s parameter (introduced in PostgreSQL 12) allows DBAs to force either always using custom plans (%s) or always using generic cached plans (%s), which is sometimes useful when dealing with parameters that have highly skewed distributions.""" % (code('WHERE customer_id = 1'), code('WHERE customer_id = 99999'), code('plan_cache_mode'), code('force_custom_plan'), code('force_generic_plan')),
 """This matters because the optimal plan for %s can differ wildly from %s depending on data distribution — the classic setup for <strong class="text-white">parameter sniffing</strong>, covered next. When the plan cache is polluted with single-use ad-hoc plans, the blunt instrument is %s, but that evicts every plan on the instance and causes a compile storm; prefer targeted fixes like forced parameterization or plan guides.""" % (code('WHERE customer_id = 1'), code('WHERE customer_id = 99999'), code('DBCC FREEPROCCACHE'))),

# 14 parameter sniffing + pg_stat_statements
("""SQL Server's equivalent challenge is <strong class="text-white">parameter sniffing</strong>. When SQL Server first compiles a plan for a stored procedure, it uses the actual parameter values at that moment to estimate cardinality, and the resulting plan is cached. If those initial values were unusual — either very selective or very broad — the cached plan may perform poorly for typical parameter values. This is one of the most common performance problems in SQL Server, and solutions range from %s on the query, to %s hints, to breaking procedures into smaller pieces. From PostgreSQL 16 onward, <strong class="text-white">query identifier</strong> tracking through %s makes it easier to identify plan instability patterns without needing to instrument the application layer.""" % (code('OPTION (RECOMPILE)'), code('OPTIMIZE FOR'), code('pg_stat_statements')),
 """SQL Server's signature plan-cache problem is <strong class="text-white">parameter sniffing</strong>. When SQL Server first compiles a plan for a stored procedure, it uses the actual parameter values at that moment to estimate cardinality, and the resulting plan is cached. If those initial values were unusual — either very selective or very broad — the cached plan may perform poorly for typical parameter values. This is one of the most common performance problems in SQL Server, and solutions range from %s on the query, to %s hints, to breaking procedures into smaller pieces. For plan instability that comes and goes, Query Store is the diagnostic tool: it captures the plan history per query so you can see exactly when the plan flipped and force the good one back.""" % (code('OPTION (RECOMPILE)'), code('OPTIMIZE FOR'))),

# 15 materialized views
("""PostgreSQL's %s is notable because it allows the view to remain queryable during the refresh — locks are minimal, and readers see the previous version of the data until the new version is ready. The %s option requires a unique index on the materialized view. SQL Server's indexed views are automatically maintained as the underlying data changes, which is both convenient and a source of overhead on write-heavy tables. The DBA must weigh the write overhead against the query performance gain.""" % (code('REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW CONCURRENTLY'), code('CONCURRENTLY')),
 """SQL Server's <strong class="text-white">indexed views</strong> are automatically maintained as the underlying data changes, which is both convenient and a source of overhead on write-heavy tables — every write to the base tables also maintains the view's clustered index. The DBA must weigh the write overhead against the query performance gain. Indexed views come with strict requirements (schema binding, deterministic expressions, and in Standard Edition the %s hint to use them), so for refresh-on-schedule semantics many shops instead maintain snapshot tables via Agent jobs — a manual materialized view with full control over refresh timing and locking.""" % code('NOEXPAND')),

# 16 ring buffers
("""<strong class="text-white">Cache eviction under memory pressure</strong> occurs when the buffer pool fills and the eviction algorithm must push out pages. In PostgreSQL, if a large ETL job or reporting query performs sequential scans over many tables, it will load millions of pages into shared buffers, potentially evicting hot pages that the OLTP workload depends on. PostgreSQL partially mitigates this through a mechanism called <strong class="text-white">ring buffers</strong> for sequential scans: large sequential scans use a small, fixed ring of buffer slots rather than populating the full shared buffer pool. This is automatic for scans over tables larger than a certain threshold (roughly 256 KB by default, controlled by %s and internal heuristics). The result is that sequential scans of large tables do not destroy the buffer pool, though they can still contribute to pressure.""" % code('effective_io_concurrency'),
 """<strong class="text-white">Cache eviction under memory pressure</strong> occurs when the buffer pool fills and the eviction algorithm must push out pages. If a large ETL job or reporting query scans a multi-gigabyte table, it will load millions of pages into the buffer pool, potentially evicting hot pages that the OLTP workload depends on. SQL Server has no ring-buffer protection for this — a big scan genuinely washes through the pool. The mitigations are architectural: route analytics to a readable secondary, reach for a columnstore index so the scan touches far fewer pages, or schedule the heavy scan for off-peak hours. Never "fix" it with %s in production — that evicts everything, including the hot pages you were trying to protect.""" % code('DBCC DROPCLEANBUFFERS')),

# 17 read replica routing
("""<strong class="text-white">Cache pollution</strong> from analytics workloads is a real problem that many teams address by routing analytical queries to a read replica. Both PostgreSQL streaming replication and SQL Server Always On Availability Groups support read-intent replicas. Directing reporting workloads to the replica not only keeps the primary's buffer pool clean but also offloads CPU and I/O from the primary entirely.""",
 """<strong class="text-white">Cache pollution</strong> from analytics workloads is a real problem that many teams address by routing analytical queries to a readable secondary. With read-only routing on the AG listener, connections marked %s land on secondaries automatically. Directing reporting workloads there not only keeps the primary's buffer pool clean but also offloads CPU and I/O from the primary entirely.""" % code('ApplicationIntent=ReadOnly')),

# 18 PgBouncer prepared statements
("""<strong class="text-white">Connection poolers and plan cache invalidation</strong> create a subtle problem. Tools like PgBouncer (for PostgreSQL) in session-mode maintain prepared statements across connection reuse, which is efficient. In transaction-mode pooling — where a client gets a backend connection only for the duration of a transaction — prepared statements cannot persist across transactions, because the client might get a different backend each time. This forces applications to use protocol-level prepared statements carefully, or to fall back to non-prepared queries, giving up plan cache benefits. When configuring a connection pooler, the DBA must understand whether the application relies on server-side prepared statements and choose the pooling mode accordingly.""",
 """<strong class="text-white">Connection pooling and the plan cache</strong> interact more benignly in SQL Server than in systems where pooling is a separate tier. Plans are cached server-side by query hash, independent of which pooled connection executed them — %s resets session state between checkouts but never evicts plans. So a well-pooled application using %s gets both benefits at once: cheap connection checkout and a warm plan cache. The thing pooling does <em>not</em> fix is cache bloat from non-parameterized ad-hoc SQL; that's a query-text problem, solved with parameterization, not with pool tuning.""" % (code('sp_reset_connection'), code('sp_executesql'))),

